# A server-only Tor: it publishes ONE onion service in front of the relay's
# websocket port and dials nothing on anyone's behalf. The client proxy the
# router uses to REACH .onion upstreams is the other torrc in this directory,
# running as its own container — one Tor with both jobs would mean a
# `restart sync` (a router.conf edit, meant to be cheap) also drops the hidden
# service's descriptor and takes the relay off Tor for minutes.

# No SOCKS port at all. This instance answers; nothing here needs to dial out
# on behalf of a local program, and a proxy nobody asked for is a proxy someone
# else can use.
SocksPort 0

# Explicit because this instance starts as root and drops to `User tor` below:
# tor's default DataDirectory is $HOME/.tor, and $HOME here is root's, so the
# default sends it to /root/.tor and it refuses to start ("Couldn't access
# private data directory"). Its own volume, alongside the service key.
DataDirectory /var/lib/tor

# The service's identity. The .onion address IS the public half of the key
# under this directory, so the directory is a named volume: lose it and the
# relay comes back at a DIFFERENT address, with every client that had the old
# one pointed at nothing. Tor creates and owns it on first start; the entrypoint
# hands it over before dropping privileges.
HiddenServiceDir /var/lib/tor/onion

# The forwarded port is NOT here: tor parses `HiddenServicePort` once, at
# startup, and refuses a hostname target outright ("Unparseable address in
# hidden service port configuration") — it wants an IP. The compose service
# name is all we have, so the entrypoint resolves it and appends the line to a
# generated copy of this file. See publish-onion.sh, which also notices when
# that IP changes under a re-created relay.

# Started as root so the entrypoint can hand the volumes over, then dropped
# here — the daemon itself never runs as root.
User tor

# To the container log, where `docker compose logs tor-onion` finds it. The
# address is echoed by the entrypoint once Tor mints it; "Bootstrapped 100%"
# is what says the descriptor can be published at all.
Log notice stdout
